{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-4-6","id":"msg_01JKT2mCM2ZQ7Bw1uTpnJ7XH","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"thinking","thinking":"I've now read the full 13-page PDF and the review context YAML. Let me organize my understanding before dispatching the two reviewers.\n\n**Paper Summary**: ContamCal is a two-stage framework for calibrating LLM benchmark scores for training data contamination. Stage 1 uses 4 detection signals (embedding similarity, Min-K++, ConStat, self-critique) aggregated via RRF to estimate item-level contamination exposure. Stage 2 fits a contamination-augmented 4PL IRT model with per-benchmark γ_k parameters and a measurement error model. \n\nThe main contributions are achieving 1.8pp MAE with 94% CI coverage, demonstrating how signal effectiveness varies across benchmark scales, and using Fisher Information analysis to establish identifiability boundaries. Looking at the experiments listed in the YAML against what's actually in the paper, I can see the stage 1 signal pipeline is covered in Table 1 and Section 4.1, the RRF aggregation ablation appears in Table 4 and Section 4.4, the simulation identifiability sweep is in Table 9 and Section 5.5, and the noise propagation analysis is also present.\n\nThe remaining experiments map to their respective tables and sections: IRT calibration and baselines in Table 5 and Section 5.2, scale bridging work in Tables 11-12 and Section 5.7, signal survival across scales in Tables 2-3 and Sections 4.2-4.3, the RL observational case study in Table 15 and Section 5.10, the 72B observational results in Table 16 and Section 5.10, closed-weight two-signal mode in Table 14 and Section 5.9, and multi-family validation in Table 13 and Section 5.8. All the experiments from the YAML are accounted for in the paper.\n\nNow I'm ready to dispatch the two reviewer agents in parallel—one focusing on methods and experiments, the other on novelty and related work—then run through the full 