{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-4-6","id":"msg_01JKT2mCM2ZQ7Bw1uTpnJ7XH","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_01WjGgFnKYdC1t8wueh5cHM2","name":"Agent","input":{"description":"R2: Novelty + Related Work review","prompt":"You are Reviewer R2 for a top ML venue review (acceptance rate ~25%, 6-point scale: 1=Strong Reject, 6=Strong Accept). Your focus areas are **Novelty/Originality** and **Related Work/Positioning**.\n\n## Paper Summary\n\n**Title**: ContamCal: Calibrating Benchmark Scores for Training Data Contamination via Contamination-Augmented Item Response Theory\n\n**Core idea**: A two-stage framework bridging contamination detection and score calibration. Stage 1 computes item-level contamination exposure via 4 detection signals (embedding similarity, Min-K++, ConStat, LLM self-critique) aggregated through RRF. Stage 2 fits a contamination-augmented 4PL IRT model with per-benchmark γ_k and a measurement error model. Key reframing: rather than trying to eliminate contamination, statistically control for it.\n\n**Key results**: 1.8pp MAE (bootstrap 95% CI [1.2, 2.4]) with 94% credible interval coverage across Qwen2.5 (0.5B-7B) and Llama-3.2-1B, 3 benchmarks, 5 dosages, 3 seeds.\n\n## Paper Structure (13 pages)\n\n### Abstract & Introduction\n- Frames contamination as a confounder to be statistically adjusted rather than eliminated\n- Draws analogy to causal inference (Rosenbaum & Rubin 1983)\n- Three contributions: (1) ContamCal framework, (2) systematic signal characterization, (3) dual-track validation\n\n### Related Work (Section 2, ~1 page)\nSix subsections:\n1. **Contamination Detection**: Min-K++ (Shi et al. 2024), ConStat (Dekoninck et al. 2024), KDS (Zhang et al. 2024), CDD (Li et al. 2024b), Yang et al. 2023 self-critique. \"These methods flag items but do not produce calibrated scores.\"\n2. **IRT for LLM Evaluation**: Lalor et al. 2024, Polo et al. 2024, White et al. 2024, Zhuang et al. 2024. \"None model contamination as a covariate.\" Builds on explanatory IRT (De Boeck & Wilson 2004, Adams et al. 1997) and item compromise (Eckerly 2017, Sinharay 2017).\n3. **Contamination Mitigation**: DCR (Dong et al. 2024), Xu et al. 2024 survey (20 strategies all fail), LiveBench (White et al. 2024). \"Our approach is complementary.\"\n4. **Calibration and Uncertainty**: Distinguished from probability calibration (Guo et al. 2017, Platt 1999). Conformal prediction (Angelopoulos & Bates 2023) discussed but IRT preferred for interpretability.\n5. **Differential Item Functioning**: γ_k conceptually related to DIF (Holland & Wainer 1993, Thissen et al. 1993, Zumbo 1999). Key distinction: DIF compares demographic subgroups; this compares contamination exposure levels of items within a single model.\n\n### Method (Section 3)\n- Stage 1: 4 signals + RRF aggregation (Cormack et al. 2009)\n- Stage 2: 4PL+γ IRT with measurement error model, per-benchmark γ_k, Fisher Information analysis for identifiability\n- Calibrated score = expected accuracy under zero exposure\n\n### Key Technical Innovations\n1. Contamination as IRT covariate (γ_k·τ_i term in difficulty)\n2. Per-benchmark γ_k (heterogeneous contamination susceptibility)\n3. Measurement error model propagating signal noise to IRT posterior\n4. Fisher Information identifiability analysis (AUC≥0.55 structural, ≥0.70 practical)\n5. Training-free RRF aggregation avoiding meta-circularity\n\n### Experiments\n- Controlled: Qwen2.5 (0.5B-3B) × 5 dosages × 3 seeds × 3 benchmarks + 7B + Llama-3.2-1B\n- 7 baselines: Raw, Remove-rescore, 2PL+γ (S1 only), Vanilla 4PL, Linear+boot, Emperor mitigation, DCR fuzzy\n- Observational: Qwen2.5-Instruct, DeepSeek-R1, Qwen2.5-72B\n- Signal analysis: AUC mapping, Min-K++ reversal, RRF ablation\n- Simulation: 120-condition identifiability sweep\n\n### References\n~28 references. Key prior work cited: Shi et al. 2024 (Min-K++), Dekoninck et al. 2024 (ConStat), Dong et al. 2024 (DCR), Lalor et al. 2024 & Polo et al. 2024 (IRT for LLMs), De Boeck & Wilson 2004 (explanatory IRT), Eckerly 2017 & Sinharay 2017 (item compromise), Holland & Wainer 1993 (DIF).\n\n### Limitations\nModel family breadth, unidimensional θ, SFT≠real contamination, local independence, closed-weight degradation, computational cost.\n\n## Your Review Task\n\nProvide a detailed review focusing on:\n\n1. **Novelty Assessment**: How novel is the core idea of using contamination as an IRT covariate? How novel are the individual components (signal fusion, measurement error model, Fisher analysis)? Is this a genuine conceptual advance or incremental combination?\n\n2. **Related Work Coverage**: Are key prior works cited? Are there important missing references? Is the positioning accurate and fair?\n\n3. **Framing and Claims**: Is the causal inference analogy appropriate? Are the three claims well-supported? Is the paper overselling or underselling?\n\n4. **Broader Impact and Significance**: Would this change how the community thinks about contamination? Is the practical impact significant?\n\n5. **Weaknesses in Positioning**: Where does the novelty argument break down? What are the strongest counterarguments?\n\n## Output Format\n\n```\n## R2 Review: Novelty & Related Work\n\n### Summary\n[2-3 sentences]\n\n### Strengths\n[Numbered list, 4-6 items]\n\n### Weaknesses\n[Numbered list, 4-6 items]\n\n### Questions for Authors\n[Numbered list, 3-5 items]\n\n### Minor Issues\n[Numbered list]\n\n### Preliminary Score: X/6\n### Confidence: X/5\n```\n"},"caller":{"type":"direct"}}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":3,"cache_creation_input_tokens":38260,"cache_read_input_tokens":22964,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":38260},"output_tokens":19,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"not_available"},"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"49fcde5e-0610-4e7e-a894-56110a3c51ee","uuid":"3961dc16-845a-419a-9f7e-3a97f394ca87"}
