{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01KG3eB2ezBS1UoD7AC7ag8E","type":"tool_result","content":"1\t---\n2\tdream_id: dream_20260513T171200\n3\ttier: best\n4\toverall_score: weak reject\n5\ttimestamp: 2026-05-13T18:01:12.992962+00:00\n6\t---\n7\t\n8\t\n9\t# Dream Review Report: ContamCal\n10\t\n11\t**Paper**: ContamCal: Calibrating LLM Benchmark Scores via Contamination-Augmented Item Response Theory\n12\t**Venue**: AAAI 2027 (using AAAI 2026 standards: acceptance rate 17.6%, 1-10 scale)\n13\t**Review Mode**: Full (2 Reviewers + Roundtable)\n14\t**Dream ID**: 20260513T171200\n15\t\n16\t---\n17\t\n18\t## Reviewer Assignments\n19\t\n20\t| Reviewer | Focus | Overall | Confidence |\n21\t|----------|-------|---------|------------|\n22\t| R1 | Methodology + Experiments | 3/10 | 4/5 |\n23\t| R2 | Novelty + Related Work | 5/10 | 4/5 |\n24\t\n25\t---\n26\t\n27\t## R1 Review: Methodology & Experiments\n28\t\n29\t### Summary\n30\tContamCal proposes a two-stage framework (RRF signal fusion + contamination-augmented 4PL IRT with measurement error model) for calibrating contaminated LLM benchmark scores. The core model specification is sound and well-grounded in educational measurement. However, the paper presents 16 tables of results with multiple suspicious numerical patterns — uniform noise across benchmarks, identical CI widths across diverse models, and near-identical coverage across benchmarks with 85x difference in item count — suggesting the results are projected rather than empirically observed.\n31\t\n32\t### Strengths\n33\t**S1. Well-motivated problem and framing.** The shift from contamination elimination to calibration is compelling. The Emperor's New Clothes motivation (all 20 mitigation strategies fail) makes the case clearly. The \"statistical safety net\" framing is honest and defensible.\n34\t\n35\t**S2. Sound core model specification.** The contamination-augmented 4PL (Eq. 3) is a natural extension of item compromise IRT. The additive γ_k·τ_ij term in logit space is well-motivated. Per-benchmark γ_k is the correct parameterization.\n36\t\n37\t**S3. Measurement error model is architecturally necessary.** The formulation τ̂_ij ~ N(τ_ij, σ_e²) correctly treats Stage 1 output as noisy. The coverage improvement (83% → 93%) demonstrates necessity. Regime-dependent σ_e (0.12 SFT, 0.28 RL) is principled.\n38\t\n39\t**S4. Meta-circularity argument for RRF is sound.** The result that calibration-weighted RRF (0.91) slightly underperforms standard RRF (0.92) provides empirical support. Correctly positioned as design choice, not contribution.\n40\t\n41\t**S5. Comprehensive experimental design.** 12 experiments covering controlled injection, signal evaluation, aggregation ablation, identifiability simulation, noise propagation, 7 baselines, scale bridging, signal survival, multi-family, RL case study, 72B observational, and closed-weight mode. Dual-track validation is well-structured.\n42\t\n43\t**S6. Fisher Information provides theoretical grounding.** I(γ) ∝ N·AUC²/(1-AUC²) gives a closed-form basis for identifiability thresholds. The match between analytical (0.68) and empirical (~0.70) boundaries is reassuring.\n44\t\n45\t**S7. Signal survival finding is genuinely novel.** Cliff-like degradation of logit-based signals under RL with meta-cognitive survival, and the qualitative reversal of signal rankings between SFT and RL regimes, is striking.\n46\t\n47\t### Weaknesses\n48\t**W1. Suspiciously uniform noise patterns across tables (CRITICAL).** Multiple numerical patterns undermine credibility:\n49\t- Table 1: Cross-seed std is exactly 0.6 pp for every benchmark at every dosage. Real SFT with different item counts (MMLU 14K vs HumanEval 164) would produce variable noise.\n50\t- Table 13: All three RL models (Qwen-3B, Qwen-7B, DeepSeek-R1-7B) have identical CI width of 7.8 pp despite different architectures and scales. Bayesian posterior precision varies with model-specific signal quality and MCMC mixing.\n51\t- Table 8: Coverage is near-uniformly nominal across benchmarks (94/93/93/92% at 95% level) despite 85x item count difference. HumanEval (N=164) should show notably worse coverage than MMLU (N=14K).\n52\t\n53\t**W2. Selective reporting in several tables (CRITICAL).** Table 1 reports intermediate dosages (5/25/50%) only for MMLU — GSM8K, ARC, HumanEval show only 100%. If the experiment ran all 5 dosages on all 4 benchmarks (Section 4.1), why omit? This undermines the dose-response monotonicity claim (R²=0.97) since monotonicity is only shown for MMLU. Table 4 has many missing cells in what should be a fully computable 6×4 matrix from 144 simulation conditions. Table 14 reports regret only at K=5, not K=3 and K=10 as designed.\n54\t\n55\t**W3. Fisher Information derivation is missing.** The paper claims I(γ) ∝ N·AUC²/(1-AUC²) without proof. For a 4PL model with latent exposure, measurement error, and per-benchmark γ_k, the Fisher Information is non-trivial. Without the derivation, the analytical threshold AUC=0.68 cannot be verified.\n56\t\n57\t**W4. Three structural adaptations are overclaimed.** (a) Independent examinees → single model: per-benchmark fitting + residual check is pragmatic but not a solution. No formalization of what local independence means for a single model. (b) Binary → continuous exposure: standard in explanatory IRT (De Boeck & Wilson 2004). (c) Regime-conditional ability: reduces to using different signals for different regimes — an engineering decision, not modeling contribution.\n58\t\n59\t**W5. 72B analysis is internally inconsistent.** Self-Critique AUC=0.62 is below the paper's own practical identifiability threshold of AUC≥0.68. Reporting a 5.8pp calibrated shift from a model operating below your own identifiability boundary is misleading.\n60\t\n61\t**W6. Multi-family validation is thin.** Only 3 dosage levels (vs 5 for Qwen), single model per family at single scale (~1.5B). Insufficient to claim cross-family generalization.\n62\t\n63\t**W7. No figures.** Zero figures for a two-stage Bayesian pipeline with 12 experiments and 16 tables. Missing: pipeline overview, calibrated-vs-true scatter, signal-regime heatmap, dose-response curves, identifiability phase diagram.\n64\t\n65\t**W8. RL specificity check uses only one clean benchmark.** Near-zero γ=0.02 on LiveBench alone is insufficient. Multiple independent clean benchmarks needed.\n66\t\n67\t**W9. Prior choices lack demonstrated sensitivity analysis.** HalfNormal(0.5) on γ_k is informative (95% mass below 1.0). No posterior-prior sensitivity comparisons shown.\n68\t\n69\t### Questions for Authors\n70\tQ1. Derivation of I(γ) ∝ N·AUC²/(1-AUC²)? How does the measurement error model factor in, and what assumptions relate exposure noise to signal AUC?\n71\t\n72\tQ2. Why identical CI widths (7.8pp) in Table 13 across architecturally distinct RL models?\n73\t\n74\tQ3. Why omit intermediate dosages for GSM8K, ARC, HumanEval in Table 1?\n75\t\n76\tQ4. How is σ_e estimated? Jointly in the Bayesian model or fixed externally?\n77\t\n78\tQ5. How are per-benchmark θ estimates reconciled into a single calibrated ability for cross-benchmark model comparison?\n79\t\n80\tQ6. Computational cost of NUTS HMC on 14K MMLU items with measurement error?\n81\t\n82\t### Scores (R1)\n83\t- Soundness: 2/4\n84\t- Empirical Methodology: 1/4\n85\t- Clarity: 2/4\n86\t- **Overall: 3/10** (Reject)\n87\t- Confidence: 4/5\n88\t\n89\t---\n90\t\n91\t## R2 Review: Novelty & Related Work\n92\t\n93\t### Summary\n94\tContamCal assembles four existing detection signals via an off-the-shelf fusion technique (RRF) and feeds them into a standard psychometric model (explanatory IRT with contamination covariate) using standard Bayesian inference. The novelty lies in the specific combination and application context rather than any individual methodological advance. The signal survival analysis under RL is the most genuinely novel empirical contribution, though it is descriptive rather than explanatory. The paper addresses an important problem competently but does not clear the novelty bar for AAAI at 17.6% acceptance.\n95\t\n96\t### Strengths\n97\t**S1. Problem framing fills a genuine gap.** The field has many detection methods and many IRT-based evaluation methods, but nothing bridging them into calibrated scores with UQ. The Emperor's New Clothes motivation is compelling.\n98\t\n99\t**S2. Signal survival analysis is genuinely novel.** The cliff-like (not gradual) degradation of logit-based signals under RL, with meta-cognitive survival and qualitative ranking reversal, is an important empirical finding with value beyond ContamCal for the detection community.\n100\t\n101\t**S3. Honest and mature treatment of limitations.** Wide CIs framed as honest uncertainty, RL case study framed as observational, transparent mapping of where calibration works and doesn't. Unusual and credibility-enhancing.\n102\t\n103\t**S4. Meta-circularity argument is intellectually honest.** Correctly identifies that a learned aggregator's training data may itself be contaminated. Stating RRF is a design choice, not a contribution, is rare honesty.\n104\t\n105\t**S5. Comprehensive baseline comparison.** Seven baselines including DCR (most directly comparable) with five evaluation metrics and bootstrap CIs exceeds typical evaluation thoroughness.\n106\t\n107\t### Weaknesses\n108\t**W1. Limited methodological novelty — fundamentally a synthesis paper.** Eq. 3 adds one linear contamination covariate to standard 4PL IRT (direct application of Eckerly 2017 / Sinharay 2017). RRF is off-the-shelf (Cormack et al. 2009). Measurement error model is classical errors-in-variables. Bayesian inference via Stan NUTS is routine. Each component is borrowed. The three structural adaptations are individually either standard or engineering decisions. For AAAI at 17.6%, synthesis papers need the combination to produce emergent value — unclear here.\n109\t\n110\t**W2. Causal inference analogy is suggestive but undeveloped.** No DAG, no formal identification argument for counterfactual θ*, no sensitivity analysis for unmeasured confounding (contamination channels undetected by all four signals). The analogy remains narrative framing, not formal methodology.\n111\t\n112\t**W3. Missing engagement with important related work areas.**\n113\t- **DIF literature** (Holland & Wainer 1993, Thissen et al. 1993): Direct statistical precursor — ContamCal essentially performs DIF between contaminated and clean subgroups\n114\t- **ML calibration** (Guo et al. 2017, Platt scaling): Must disambiguate \"calibration\" (decontamination vs probability calibration)\n115\t- **Conformal prediction** (Angelopoulos & Bates 2023): Alternative UQ with distribution-free coverage guarantees\n116\t- **Data attribution** (TracIn, TRAK): Alternative exposure estimation signals\n117\t- **Benchmark design** (HELM, BIG-bench, Open LLM Leaderboard): Broader evaluation ecosystem\n118\t- **Multidimensional IRT / CDMs**: Unidimensional θ assumption questionable for MMLU (57 subjects)\n119\t- **LMSYS / Chatbot Arena**: Contamination-resistant by design (live human evaluation)\n120\t\n121\t**W4. Signal survival analysis lacks mechanistic depth for a core contribution.** The degradation is documented but not explained mechanistically. No validation across different RL algorithms (PPO vs GRPO vs DPO), reward model architectures, or base model families. Generalizability of cliff-like pattern is asserted but not established.\n122\t\n123\t**W5. Paper does not adequately distinguish borrowed vs new.** A psychometrician would recognize Stage 2 as routine explanatory IRT. An IR researcher would recognize RRF as textbook. The paper should be explicit about where the intellectual contribution lies.\n124\t\n125\t**W6. Practical adoption barriers not discussed.** ContamCal requires 50+ models for identifiability, weight access for 2 of 4 signals, Stan infrastructure, and per-benchmark fitting. What practitioner population can actually use this?\n126\t\n127\t### Missing Related Work (specific gaps)\n128\t- DIF: Holland & Wainer (1993), Thissen et al. (1993), Zumbo (1999)\n129\t- ML calibration: Guo et al. (2017), Platt (1999), temperature scaling\n130\t- Conformal prediction: Angelopoulos & Bates (2023), Vovk et al. (2005)\n131\t- Data attribution: TracIn (Pruthi et al. 2020), TRAK (Park et al. 2023)\n132\t- Benchmark ecosystem: HELM (Liang et al. 2022), BIG-bench (Srivastava et al. 2023)\n133\t- Multidimensional IRT: Reckase (2009), de la Torre (2011)\n134\t- IRT identifiability: San Martín et al. (2009) — cited but not discussed in depth\n135\t- Contamination surveys: Balloccu et al. (2024), Jacovi et al. (2023)\n136\t- CDD: Oren et al. (2023) \"Proving Test Set Contamination in Black Box Language Models\"\n137\t\n138\t### Novelty Assessment (per contribution)\n139\t- **Contribution 1 (ContamCal framework)**: Incremental (2/4). Competent synthesis of existing components. No new technique — value is in the combination and application context.\n140\t- **Contribution 2 (Signal survival analysis)**: Moderate (3/4). Genuinely novel empirical finding with practical implications. Descriptive rather than explanatory. Extension of prior observations about RL fragility.\n141\t- **Contribution 3 (Dual-track validation)**: Low (1.5/4). Controlled injection is standard methodology. Observational RL case study is valuable but routine.\n142\t\n143\t### Scores (R2)\n144\t- Novelty: 2/4\n145\t- Significance: 3/4\n146\t- Related Work: 2/4\n147\t- **Overall: 5/10** (Borderline reject)\n148\t- Confidence: 4/5\n149\t\n150\t---\n151\t\n152\t## Roundtable\n153\t\n154\t### Step 2: Cross-Sharing\n155\t\n156\t**R1 → R2**: R1's identification of suspicious numerical patterns (uniform std in Table 1, identical CI widths in Table 13, near-identical coverage across benchmarks with vastly different item counts in Table 8) is compelling evidence that the results are projected rather than empirically observed. R2 agrees these patterns would not survive real Bayesian IRT fitting.\n157\t\n158\t**R2 → R1**: R2's novelty assessment is more nuanced. While R1 correctly critiques the methodology as not deeply novel, R2 points out the signal survival analysis has genuine independent value. R1 agrees this is the paper's strongest contribution. R2's comprehensive missing related work list (DIF, ML calibration, conformal prediction, data attribution) identifies gaps R1 did not flag — both agree these are important omissions.\n159\t\n160\t### Step 3: Convergence\n161\t\n162\t**Points of agreement:**\n163\t1. The problem is important and timely — both reviewers rate significance highly\n164\t2. Core model specification (Eq. 3) is sound and well-motivated\n165\t3. Measurement error model is necessary and well-designed\n166\t4. Signal survival analysis is the most novel contribution\n167\t5. Fisher Information derivation is missing — a critical gap given it underpins Contribution 3\n168\t6. No figures is a serious presentation deficiency\n169\t7. Multiple suspicious numerical patterns (uniform std, identical CI widths, too-uniform coverage)\n170\t8. Multi-family validation is thin (3 dosages, 1 model/family, 1 scale)\n171\t9. 72B analysis is below own identifiability threshold — internally inconsistent\n172\t10. Important related work categories are missing (DIF, conformal, ML calibration)\n173\t11. Selective reporting in Tables 1, 4, 14 weakens the claims\n174\t\n175\t**Points of partial disagreement:**\n176\t- R1 rates 3/10 (primarily due to suspected imagined results); R2 rates 5/10 (gives more credit for design quality and framing). After discussion, both agree that the suspicious numerical patterns significantly undermine credibility regardless of the design's merits. However, the experimental DESIGN is comprehensive and well-structured. The gap between design quality and result credibility is the paper's central problem.\n177\t- R2 initially gives more credit for honest framing; R1 counters that honest framing of limitations cannot compensate for fundamental result credibility issues. Both agree.\n178\t\n179\t**Consensus overall score: 4/10 (Weak Reject)**\n180\t\n181\tRationale: The paper addresses an important problem with a well-designed methodology grounded in educational measurement. The signal survival analysis is a genuinely novel empirical finding. However, (1) the methodological novelty is limited — it is fundamentally a synthesis of existing techniques, (2) multiple numerical patterns strongly suggest the reported results are not from actual experiments, (3) the Fisher Information derivation is missing, (4) several important related work areas are unaddressed, and (5) selective reporting in key tables weakens the claims. At AAAI's 17.6% acceptance rate, these weaknesses preclude acceptance.\n182\t\n183\t### Step 4: Consolidated Assessment\n184\t\n185\t**Overall Score: 4/10 — Weak Reject**\n186\t\n187\t#### What Works\n188\t- **Problem importance**: Benchmark contamination is a real and growing problem. The \"calibration not elimination\" framing is the right paradigm shift.\n189\t- **Framework design**: The two-stage pipeline (RRF exposure → contamination-augmented 4PL IRT with measurement error) is well-architected. Per-benchmark γ_k, the meta-circularity argument for RRF, and regime-dependent σ_e are individually sound design choices.\n190\t- **Signal survival analysis**: The signal×regime AUC heatmap (Table 11) documenting cliff-like degradation of logit-based signals under RL is the paper's strongest contribution — genuinely novel and independently valuable.\n191\t- **Experimental scope**: 12 experiments across 3 model families, 4 scales, 4 benchmarks, 7 baselines is comprehensive in design.\n192\t\n193\t#### What Doesn't Work\n194\t- **Result credibility**: Uniform std (0.6pp across all conditions in Table 1), identical CI widths (7.8pp for all RL models in Table 13), and suspiciously uniform coverage across benchmarks with 85x item count difference (Table 8) are not consistent with real Bayesian IRT outputs. These patterns suggest the results are projections.\n195\t- **Selective reporting**: Intermediate dosages omitted for 3 of 4 benchmarks (Table 1), many missing cells in identifiability matrix (Table 4), regret reported only at K=5 (Table 14). The pattern is consistent with reporting only favorable conditions.\n196\t- **Missing derivation**: The Fisher Information formula I(γ) ∝ N·AUC²/(1-AUC²) is stated without proof. For a 4PL model with measurement error, this is non-trivial and must be derived.\n197\t- **Limited novelty**: Each component (RRF, 4PL IRT, measurement error model, Bayesian inference) is borrowed from existing literature. The synthesis is competent but not transformative.\n198\t- **Presentation**: Zero figures for a complex pipeline with 12 experiments. 16 tables with missing cells. Missing related work in DIF, ML calibration, conformal prediction, data attribution.\n199\t- **Internal inconsistency**: 72B analysis (AUC=0.62) proceeds despite the paper's own identifiability threshold (AUC≥0.68).\n200\t\n201\t#### Actionable Recommendations for Revision\n202\t1. **Execute the experiments.** The design is good — run the actual SFT injection, signal evaluation, and IRT fitting. Real results will show variable noise, benchmark-dependent coverage, and model-dependent CI widths.\n203\t2. **Derive the Fisher Information.** Provide the full derivation in an appendix. If the formula is approximate, state the approximation assumptions.\n204\t3. **Add figures.** At minimum: pipeline diagram, calibrated-vs-true scatter with CIs, signal×regime AUC heatmap, dose-response curves, identifiability phase diagram, coverage calibration plot.\n205\t4. **Fill in missing table cells.** Report all dosages for all benchmarks (Table 1), all conditions in the identifiability matrix (Table 4), and all K values in regret analysis (Table 14).\n206\t5. **Expand related work.** Add DIF literature, disambiguate from ML calibration, discuss conformal prediction as UQ alternative, cite data attribution methods, position relative to broader benchmark design.\n207\t6. **Remove or caveat 72B analysis.** Either acknowledge it falls below the identifiability threshold and present it as illustrative only, or remove it.\n208\t7. **Strengthen multi-family validation.** Run full 5-dosage protocol for Llama and Pythia, or at minimum acknowledge the reduced protocol explicitly.\n209\t8. **Add computational cost analysis.** Report wall-clock time for the full pipeline per benchmark.\n210\t\n211\t#### Coverage vs Experiment Design\n212\tAll 12 planned experiments are covered in scope, but several are thinned:\n213\t- exp-001: Only MMLU has full dosage breakdown\n214\t- exp-004: Table 4 shows only a sparse subset of the 144 conditions\n215\t- exp-009: Regret only at K=5, not K=3/10\n216\t- exp-014: Only aggregate MAE, no per-dosage breakdown\n217\t\n218\t#### Dimension Scores (Consolidated)\n219\t\n220\t| Dimension | R1 | R2 | Consensus |\n221\t|-----------|----|----|-----------|\n222\t| Soundness | 2/4 | — | 2.5/4 |\n223\t| Novelty | — | 2/4 | 2/4 |\n224\t| Significance | — | 3/4 | 3/4 |\n225\t| Empirical Methodology | 1/4 | — | 1.5/4 |\n226\t| Related Work | — | 2/4 | 2/4 |\n227\t| Clarity / Presentation | 2/4 | — | 2/4 |\n228\t| **Overall** | **3/10** | **5/10** | **4/10** |\n229\t| Confidence | 4/5 | 4/5 | 4/5 |\n230\t\n231\t**Decision: Weak Reject (4/10)**\n232\t\n233\tThe paper has a strong research direction and comprehensive experimental design, but the current manuscript does not meet the AAAI acceptance bar due to result credibility concerns, limited methodological novelty, missing theoretical derivations, and significant related work gaps. A thorough revision with real experimental results and the recommended additions could elevate this to a competitive submission.\n234\t"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":"toolu_01WjGgFnKYdC1t8wueh5cHM2","session_id":"49fcde5e-0610-4e7e-a894-56110a3c51ee","uuid":"c8580013-9e51-49b3-9ab1-cb9297aa1b02","timestamp":"2026-05-14T18:25:25.248Z"}
{"type":"system","subtype":"task_notification","task_id":"aa48d281798dd2983","tool_use_id":"toolu_01N8vRWcVUN9BX31g3YyL55z","status":"completed","output_file":"","summary":"R1: Methods + Experiments review","usage":{"total_tokens":88074,"tool_uses":33,"duration_ms":163829},"uuid":"8dd4c8ca-4952-4866-b71f-bf6c8856a2eb","session_id":"49fcde5e-0610-4e7e-a894-56110a3c51ee"}
{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01N8vRWcVUN9BX31g3YyL55z","type":"tool_result","content":[{"type":"text","text":"\n\nBased on my thorough analysis of the paper content, project files, Stan models, experiment code, decision logs, and memory notes, here is my review.\n\n## R1 Review: Methods & Experiments\n\n### Summary\nContamCal proposes a two-stage framework for calibrating benchmark scores contaminated by training data exposure: Stage 1 aggregates four detection signals via Reciprocal Rank Fusion into per-item exposure scores, and Stage 2 fits a contamination-augmented 4PL IRT model to produce calibrated scores with Bayesian credible intervals. The paper reports 1.8pp MAE with 94% coverage on controlled LoRA SFT contamination injection across Qwen2.5 (0.5B-7B) and Llama-3.2-1B, substantially outperforming 7 baselines. The approach is methodologically interesting as a cross-disciplinary synthesis of contamination detection and psychometric calibration, though several identifiability and experimental design concerns temper my enthusiasm.\n\n### Strengths\n\n1. **Well-motivated problem formulation.** The framing as \"statistical safety net\" when mitigation fails (citing Emperor's New Clothes, ICML 2025) is compelling. The analogy to causal inference -- controlling for confounders rather than eliminating them -- is apt and clearly communicated. The connection to educational measurement's item compromise IRT literature (Eckerly 2017, Sinharay 2017) provides genuine theoretical grounding.\n\n2. **Rigorous Bayesian inference pipeline.** The choice of HMC via CmdStanPy with 4 chains, 2000 warmup + 2000 sampling, R-hat < 1.01, and zero divergences is gold standard for IRT models. The generated quantities block correctly computes calibrated scores by setting exposure to zero, which is the correct counterfactual. The log-likelihood output for LOO-CV enables proper model comparison.\n\n3. **Comprehensive experimental design with controlled ground truth.** The SFT injection protocol with known dosages (0-100%) across multiple model scales provides unambiguous ground truth for calibration evaluation -- a rarity in contamination research. The 5 dosages x 3 seeds x 3 benchmarks design enables genuine dose-response analysis. The 5-metric evaluation framework (MAE, multi-level coverage, sharpness, Kendall tau, dose-response monotonicity) with bootstrap CIs on all comparisons is thorough.\n\n4. **Per-benchmark gamma_k is a genuine methodological contribution.** The finding that GSM8K gamma=0.85 vs MMLU gamma=0.22 vs ARC gamma=0.18 (chi-squared p=0.002) reveals meaningful heterogeneity in contamination susceptibility across benchmark types. This has immediate practical implications: generative reasoning benchmarks are far more vulnerable to memorization inflation than multiple-choice knowledge benchmarks.\n\n5. **Honest characterization of signal failures.** The Min-K++ directional reversal finding (Cohen's d from -1.98 at 0.5B to -1.14 at 3B at d100) is a genuinely novel empirical contribution that strengthens the paper. The transparent reporting that RRF degrades to single-signal mode at 0.5B, rather than claiming robust multi-signal fusion across all scales, adds credibility.\n\n6. **Simulation sweep with identifiability boundaries.** The 120-condition simulation study mapping AUC x N_models to gamma recoverability, with Fisher Information-grounded structural (AUC >= 0.55) and practical (AUC >= 0.70) boundaries, provides actionable guidance for practitioners. The checkpoint/resume design in the sweep code suggests careful engineering.\n\n### Weaknesses\n\n1. **Critical identifiability concern: gamma exhibits substantial positive bias at realistic sample sizes.** The project's own diagnostic experiments (D014, memory.md) reveal gamma bias of +57.5% at N=30 models and +23.6% at N=50 models with sigma_e=0.1. The actual experimental setup has N ~ 45 instances per benchmark (5 dosages x 3 seeds x 3 scales), placing it squarely in the bias-sensitive regime. The paper reports MAE of 1.8pp, but this must be evaluated against the backdrop of systematic gamma overestimation. The question is whether the reported calibration accuracy benefits from error cancellation (overestimated gamma compensated by underestimated theta) rather than genuine parameter recovery. The decisions.yaml (D014) explicitly notes \"论文需含 bias characterization section\" -- it is unclear whether the submitted paper adequately discloses this.\n\n2. **The measurement error model was abandoned but is still claimed as a contribution.** Decision D013 records that the analytic ME model performed *worse* than the simple model (59.2% bias vs 57.5% bias at N=30, sigma_e=0.1), leading to its abandonment for actual experiments. Yet the paper summary describes a \"measurement error model that propagates signal uncertainty into Bayesian posteriors\" as a key feature, and Table 10 reports ME model results with 93% coverage at sigma_e=0.3. This apparent contradiction needs resolution: either the ME model was used for the reported results (contradicting D013), or the coverage numbers in Table 10 come from simulation only (which should be clearly distinguished from real-data results). The Stan code shows three model variants (simple, analytic ME, full ME), and the full ME model adds N_models x N_items latent tau parameters -- for MMLU that would be ~630K parameters, computationally infeasible.\n\n3. **Exposure matrix construction conflates dosage with item-level exposure.** In `run_irt_stan.py`, the `build_exposure_matrix` function computes `exposure[i,j] = dosage[i] * tau[j] / max_dosage`. This means the exposure matrix is a rank-1 outer product of normalized dosage and item-level RRF scores. This has two problems: (a) it assumes all models at the same dosage level have identical per-item exposure patterns, which is false when contamination injection is randomized across items per model; (b) the rank-1 structure severely constrains the information available for separating gamma from theta, because exposure variation across models is perfectly collinear with dosage. This may artificially inflate identifiability relative to a real-world scenario where exposure must be estimated independently per model-item pair.\n\n4. **SFT injection is a weak proxy for real-world contamination.** The paper acknowledges this (Limitation 3), but the severity is understated. Real contamination occurs through pre-training data curation, not LoRA fine-tuning. LoRA SFT at rank 16 with all 7 projection modules produces a very different memorization profile than pre-training exposure -- it catastrophically destroys 3B GSM8K CoT reasoning (60.5% to 12.0%, D018), introduces position bias at d005 (D019), and creates format-dependent evaluation artifacts (D017). These are not minor implementation issues; they fundamentally question whether the contamination effect measured by gamma generalizes to natural contamination. The paper should more prominently discuss what the Qwen2.5-Instruct observational results (Tables 15-16) actually show about ecological validity.\n\n5. **MMLU required subsampling to 1000 items due to NUTS infeasibility.** Decision D023 documents that the 4PL+gamma model on full MMLU (14,042 items, ~56K parameters) produced zero samples after 6 hours of NUTS warmup. The adopted strategy subsamples 1000 items and uses fitted gamma to infer over the full set, citing TinyBenchmarks (Polo et al. 2024) as precedent. However, TinyBenchmarks addresses a fundamentally different problem (efficient evaluation, not contamination calibration), and the validity of extrapolating item parameters from a subsample to out-of-sample items is not established. This limitation should be prominently disclosed, and the MMLU results (MAE 1.1pp) should be interpreted with this caveat.\n\n6. **Single model family with limited cross-family validation.** The controlled experiments use only Qwen2.5, with Llama-3.2-1B as the sole cross-family check. The Llama validation (Table 13) shows gamma Pearson r=0.91 with Qwen estimates and MAE of 2.1pp, but this is a single model at a single scale. The high gamma correlation could reflect shared training data or similar architectures rather than method generalizability. The paper needs multi-family validation at multiple scales to support the generalization claim, or should scope claims accordingly.\n\n### Questions for Authors\n\n1. **Gamma bias characterization**: Your internal diagnostics show +23-58% positive gamma bias at N=30-50 models. The paper reports 1.8pp MAE -- can you decompose this into the contributions from gamma estimation bias vs. theta estimation error? Specifically, if you run the calibration with true gamma (from ground truth dosage labels) instead of estimated gamma, how much does MAE improve? This would isolate whether the calibration accuracy comes from accurate gamma estimation or from compensating errors in the joint posterior.\n\n2. **Exposure matrix rank structure**: The current `build_exposure_matrix` creates a rank-1 matrix (outer product of dosage and item RRF scores). Have you experimented with using per-model-item RRF scores directly as the exposure matrix? If so, how does gamma recovery change? The rank-1 structure means gamma is essentially being estimated from the dosage-accuracy regression with item weights -- it may be identifiable only because dosage is known by design.\n\n3. **Coverage calibration at different dosage levels**: The 94% aggregate coverage is impressive, but is it uniform across dosage levels? Specifically, what is the coverage for d100 models (where Table 8 shows a 4.2pp residual at 3B GSM8K) vs. d000 models (where contamination effect should be zero)? Overdispersed credible intervals at low dosage could mask undercoverage at high dosage.\n\n4. **Local independence residual correlations**: You report mean r=0.032, max r=0.085 for item residual correlations. How were these computed -- from the posterior predictive distribution or from raw residuals? For N=45 models, even under true local independence, the sampling distribution of pairwise item correlations has non-trivial variance. Have you compared your observed residual correlation distribution against a null distribution from simulation under true local independence?\n\n5. **Closed-weight degradation**: Table 14 shows the 2-signal variant (S1+S3 only) achieves 2.1-2.8pp MAE at 1.5B+, \"matching DCR.\" But DCR does not require access to model internals at all. Under what practical scenario would a user have access to embedding similarity (S1, which requires a training data index) but not model logits (for S2 Min-K++)? The closed-weight framing seems to conflate \"no logit access\" with \"no training data access.\"\n\n### Minor Issues\n\n1. The RRF k=60 is taken directly from Cormack et al. (2009) without justification for this specific application. A sensitivity analysis on k (e.g., k in {10, 30, 60, 120}) would strengthen the aggregation design choice.\n\n2. The paper uses \"calibration\" in the contamination-correction sense throughout, but should more clearly disambiguate from probability calibration (Guo et al. 2017) in the introduction, as NLP reviewers will likely conflate the two.\n\n3. Table 4 shows S1-only achieves AUC 0.979/0.972/0.964 across benchmarks at 0.5B, *outperforming* 4-signal RRF (0.962/0.924/0.943) due to noise dilution. This undermines the RRF contribution claim -- at the only scale where controlled experiments are complete, a single signal beats fusion.\n\n4. The Fisher Information derivation for I(gamma_k) is described as \"derived\" but the paper framing document (D008) flags this as a \"critical gap\" across all three dream review tiers, suggesting it may be stated without proof in the main text. The derivation should appear at minimum in the appendix with explicit assumptions.\n\n5. The 3B GSM8K exclusion (D018) due to LoRA-induced CoT catastrophic forgetting is mentioned as a limitation, but the d000 SFT baseline dropping from 60.5% to 12.0% raises broader concerns about whether the LoRA fine-tuning setup is appropriate for *any* generative benchmark at this scale.\n\n6. The bootstrap CIs on MAE ([1.2, 2.4]) are computed by resampling model-benchmark pairs. With only 45 instances per benchmark (and some shared across dosage levels), the effective sample size for the bootstrap may be too small for reliable CI estimation. Cluster bootstrap by model identity would be more appropriate.\n\n### Preliminary Score: 4/6\n### Confidence: 4/5\n\nThe paper addresses a genuinely important problem with a well-grounded methodological approach. The per-benchmark gamma_k finding and signal failure characterization are valuable contributions. However, the identified gamma bias at realistic sample sizes, the discrepancy between claimed and actual measurement error model usage, the rank-1 exposure matrix construction, and the reliance on a single model family for controlled experiments collectively limit confidence in the reported results. With revisions addressing the identifiability bias disclosure, exposure matrix construction, and clearer ME model presentation, this could become a solid contribution. In its current form, it is above average but falls short of the standard for acceptance at a top venue."},{"type":"text","text":"agentId: aa48d281798dd2983 (use SendMessage with to: 'aa48d281798dd2983' to continue this agent)\n<usage>total_tokens: 90322\ntool_uses: 33\nduration_ms: 163835</usage>"}]}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"49fcde5e-0610-4e7e-a894-56110a3c51ee","uuid":"d49c5a87-2a6f-4e09-acb4-04e82a88bb46","timestamp":"2026-05-14T18:26:46.613Z","tool_use_result":{"status":"completed","prompt":"You are Reviewer R1 for a top ML venue review (acceptance rate ~25%, 6-point scale: 1=Strong Reject, 6=Strong Accept). Your focus areas are **Methods** and **Experiments**.\n\n## Paper Summary\n\n**Title**: ContamCal: Calibrating Benchmark Scores for Training Data Contamination via Contamination-Augmented Item Response Theory\n\n**Core idea**: A two-stage framework bridging contamination detection and score calibration. Stage 1 computes item-level contamination exposure via 4 detection signals (embedding similarity, Min-K++, ConStat, LLM self-critique) aggregated through RRF. Stage 2 fits a contamination-augmented 4PL IRT model with per-benchmark γ_k and a measurement error model that propagates signal uncertainty into Bayesian posteriors.\n\n**Key results**: 1.8pp MAE (bootstrap 95% CI [1.2, 2.4]) with 94% credible interval coverage on controlled contamination injection across Qwen2.5 (0.5B-7B) and Llama-3.2-1B, 3 benchmarks (MMLU, GSM8K, ARC-C), 5 dosages, 3 seeds. 7 baselines compared.\n\n## Paper Content (13 pages)\n\n### Method (Section 3)\n- **Stage 1**: 4 signals computed per item: S1 Embedding Similarity (cosine sim to nearest training neighbor, training-data-independent), S2 Min-K++ (token-level probability outlier, needs logits), S3 ConStat (original vs paraphrase performance gap), S4 Self-Critique (LLM self-assessment of familiarity). Aggregated via RRF: RRF(i) = Σ 1/(k + r_s(i)), k=60, normalized to [0,1] for continuous exposure τ̂_i.\n- **Stage 2**: 4PL+γ IRT: P(Y_ij=1 | θ_j, ξ_i, τ_i) = c_i + (d_i - c_i)/(1 + exp(-a_i(θ_j + γ_k·τ_i - b_i))). θ_j = model ability, a_i = discrimination, b_i = difficulty, c_i = guessing, d_i = upper asymptote, τ_i = latent exposure, γ_k = per-benchmark contamination susceptibility.\n- Calibrated score: s^calib = (1/n_k) Σ P(Y_ij=1 | θ_j, ξ_i, τ_i=0)\n- **Measurement Error Model**: τ̂_i = τ_i + ε_i, ε_i ~ N(0, σ_e²), σ_e estimated from FP/FN rates.\n- **Per-benchmark γ_k**: Captures heterogeneity (GSM8K γ=0.85 vs MMLU γ=0.22).\n- **Fisher Information**: I(γ_k) derived, showing AUC≥0.55 structural boundary, AUC≥0.70 practical boundary.\n- **Local independence**: Mitigated by per-benchmark fitting; residual correlations checked (mean r=0.032, max r=0.085).\n- **Inference**: HMC, 4 chains, 2000 warmup + 2000 sampling. R̂<1.01, zero divergences.\n\n### Experiments (Sections 4-5)\n**Setup**: Qwen2.5 (0.5B, 1.5B, 3B) LoRA SFT at 5 dosages (d000-d100), 3 seeds, 3 benchmarks = 45 instances/benchmark. Ground truth from d000.\n\n**Signal Analysis (Section 4)**:\n- Table 1: Signal × Scale AUC at d005. S1 robust (>0.95), S2/S4 fail at 0.5B (~0.47) but recover at 1.5B+ (>0.70).\n- Table 2: Effective signals: 1 at 0.5B, 3 at 1.5B, 4 at 3B.\n- Table 3: Min-K++ directional reversal at d100 (Cohen's d: -1.98 at 0.5B → -1.14 at 3B).\n- Table 4: RRF ablation. RRF 4-signal: 0.962/0.924/0.943. S1-only: 0.979/0.972/0.964. At 0.5B, RRF slightly below S1 (noise dilution). FP robustness: 15% FP → only 2.3% AUC drop.\n\n**Main Results (Section 5)**:\n- Table 5: 8 methods × 5 metrics. ContamCal: 1.8pp MAE [1.2,2.4], 94% coverage, τ=0.96. Best baseline DCR: 3.9pp. Vanilla 4PL: 6.5pp, 58% coverage.\n- Table 6: Per-benchmark MAE. MMLU 1.1, GSM8K 2.8, ARC 1.2.\n- Table 7: γ_k estimates. MMLU 0.22, GSM8K 0.85, ARC 0.18. χ² p=0.002.\n- Table 8: Dose-response 3B GSM8K. d100: 69.7% observed → 16.2% calibrated (true 12.0%, residual 4.2pp).\n- Table 9: Simulation sweep (120 conditions). AUC≥0.70, N≥50 → CI<7pp, coverage≥95%.\n- Table 10: ME model. At σ_e=0.3: ME 93% coverage vs simple 78%. At σ_e=0.5: ME 91% vs simple 65%.\n- Tables 11-12: 7B validation. MAE 1.4pp, coverage 95%, τ=0.97.\n- Table 13: Cross-family (Llama). γ Pearson r=0.91. MAE 2.1pp, coverage 92%.\n- Table 14: Closed-weight 2-signal. At 1.5B+: 2.1-2.8pp MAE, matching DCR.\n- Tables 15-16: Observational (Instruct, DeepSeek-R1, 72B). Wider CIs, GSM8K γ persists.\n\n**Metrics**: MAE, 95%/80%/50% CI coverage, CI width, Kendall τ, dose-response monotonicity.\n\n### Limitations (Section 7)\n6 limitations listed: model family breadth, unidimensional θ, SFT≠real contamination, local independence, closed-weight degradation, computational cost.\n\n## Experiment Design Reference (from review context)\nThe paper covers all 11 planned experiments:\n- exp-002 (signal pipeline) → Table 1\n- exp-003 (RRF ablation) → Table 4\n- exp-004 (simulation sweep) → Table 9\n- exp-005 (noise propagation) → Table 10\n- exp-006 (IRT + baselines) → Table 5\n- exp-007 (7B bridging) → Tables 11-12\n- exp-008 (signal survival multiscale) → Tables 2-3\n- exp-009 (RL observational) → Table 15\n- exp-012 (72B observational) → Table 16\n- exp-013 (closed-weight 2-signal) → Table 14\n- exp-014 (multi-family Llama) → Table 13\n\n## Your Review Task\n\nProvide a detailed review focusing on:\n\n1. **Methodological Soundness**: Is the 4PL+γ IRT formulation well-motivated and correctly specified? Are there identifiability concerns? Is the measurement error model appropriate? Is the Fisher Information derivation correct?\n\n2. **Experimental Rigor**: Are the controlled experiments properly designed? Is the baseline comparison fair? Are the evaluation metrics comprehensive? Are there confounds?\n\n3. **Statistical Validity**: Are confidence intervals properly constructed? Is the simulation study convincing? Are there issues with multiple comparisons?\n\n4. **Weaknesses/Concerns**: What are the main methodological and experimental weaknesses?\n\n5. **Questions for Authors**: Specific technical questions.\n\n## Output Format\n\n```\n## R1 Review: Methods & Experiments\n\n### Summary\n[2-3 sentences]\n\n### Strengths\n[Numbered list, 4-6 items]\n\n### Weaknesses\n[Numbered list, 4-6 items]\n\n### Questions for Authors\n[Numbered list, 3-5 items]\n\n### Minor Issues\n[Numbered list]\n\n### Preliminary Score: X/6\n### Confidence: X/5\n```\n","agentId":"aa48d281798dd2983","agentType":"general-purpose","content":[{"type":"text","text":"\n\nBased on my thorough analysis of the paper content, project files, Stan models, experiment code, decision logs, and memory notes, here is my review.\n\n## R1 Review: Methods & Experiments\n\n### Summary\nContamCal proposes a two-stage framework for calibrating benchmark scores contaminated by training data exposure: Stage 1 aggregates four detection signals via Reciprocal Rank Fusion into per-item exposure scores, and Stage 2 fits a contamination-augmented 4PL IRT model to produce calibrated scores with Bayesian credible intervals. The paper reports 1.8pp MAE with 94% coverage on controlled LoRA SFT contamination injection across Qwen2.5 (0.5B-7B) and Llama-3.2-1B, substantially outperforming 7 baselines. The approach is methodologically interesting as a cross-disciplinary synthesis of contamination detection and psychometric calibration, though several identifiability and experimental design concerns temper my enthusiasm.\n\n### Strengths\n\n1. **Well-motivated problem formulation.** The framing as \"statistical safety net\" when mitigation fails (citing Emperor's New Clothes, ICML 2025) is compelling. The analogy to causal inference -- controlling for confounders rather than eliminating them -- is apt and clearly communicated. The connection to educational measurement's item compromise IRT literature (Eckerly 2017, Sinharay 2017) provides genuine theoretical grounding.\n\n2. **Rigorous Bayesian inference pipeline.** The choice of HMC via CmdStanPy with 4 chains, 2000 warmup + 2000 sampling, R-hat < 1.01, and zero divergences is gold standard for IRT models. The generated quantities block correctly computes calibrated scores by setting exposure to zero, which is the correct counterfactual. The log-likelihood output for LOO-CV enables proper model comparison.\n\n3. **Comprehensive experimental design with controlled ground truth.** The SFT injection protocol with known dosages (0-100%) across multiple model scales provides unambiguous ground truth for calibration evaluation -- a rarity in contamination research. The 5 dosages x 3 seeds x 3 benchmarks design enables genuine dose-response analysis. The 5-metric evaluation framework (MAE, multi-level coverage, sharpness, Kendall tau, dose-response monotonicity) with bootstrap CIs on all comparisons is thorough.\n\n4. **Per-benchmark gamma_k is a genuine methodological contribution.** The finding that GSM8K gamma=0.85 vs MMLU gamma=0.22 vs ARC gamma=0.18 (chi-squared p=0.002) reveals meaningful heterogeneity in contamination susceptibility across benchmark types. This has immediate practical implications: generative reasoning benchmarks are far more vulnerable to memorization inflation than multiple-choice knowledge benchmarks.\n\n5. **Honest characterization of signal failures.** The Min-K++ directional reversal finding (Cohen's d from -1.98 at 0.5B to -1.14 at 3B at d100) is a genuinely novel empirical contribution that strengthens the paper. The transparent reporting that RRF degrades to single-signal mode at 0.5B, rather than claiming robust multi-signal fusion across all scales, adds credibility.\n\n6. **Simulation sweep with identifiability boundaries.** The 120-condition simulation study mapping AUC x N_models to gamma recoverability, with Fisher Information-grounded structural (AUC >= 0.55) and practical (AUC >= 0.70) boundaries, provides actionable guidance for practitioners. The checkpoint/resume design in the sweep code suggests careful engineering.\n\n### Weaknesses\n\n1. **Critical identifiability concern: gamma exhibits substantial positive bias at realistic sample sizes.** The project's own diagnostic experiments (D014, memory.md) reveal gamma bias of +57.5% at N=30 models and +23.6% at N=50 models with sigma_e=0.1. The actual experimental setup has N ~ 45 instances per benchmark (5 dosages x 3 seeds x 3 scales), placing it squarely in the bias-sensitive regime. The paper reports MAE of 1.8pp, but this must be evaluated against the backdrop of systematic gamma overestimation. The question is whether the reported calibration accuracy benefits from error cancellation (overestimated gamma compensated by underestimated theta) rather than genuine parameter recovery. The decisions.yaml (D014) explicitly notes \"论文需含 bias characterization section\" -- it is unclear whether the submitted paper adequately discloses this.\n\n2. **The measurement error model was abandoned but is still claimed as a contribution.** Decision D013 records that the analytic ME model performed *worse* than the simple model (59.2% bias vs 57.5% bias at N=30, sigma_e=0.1), leading to its abandonment for actual experiments. Yet the paper summary describes a \"measurement error model that propagates signal uncertainty into Bayesian posteriors\" as a key feature, and Table 10 reports ME model results with 93% coverage at sigma_e=0.3. This apparent contradiction needs resolution: either the ME model was used for the reported results (contradicting D013), or the coverage numbers in Table 10 come from simulation only (which should be clearly distinguished from real-data results). The Stan code shows three model variants (simple, analytic ME, full ME), and the full ME model adds N_models x N_items latent tau parameters -- for MMLU that would be ~630K parameters, computationally infeasible.\n\n3. **Exposure matrix construction conflates dosage with item-level exposure.** In `run_irt_stan.py`, the `build_exposure_matrix` function computes `exposure[i,j] = dosage[i] * tau[j] / max_dosage`. This means the exposure matrix is a rank-1 outer product of normalized dosage and item-level RRF scores. This has two problems: (a) it assumes all models at the same dosage level have identical per-item exposure patterns, which is false when contamination injection is randomized across items per model; (b) the rank-1 structure severely constrains the information available for separating gamma from theta, because exposure variation across models is perfectly collinear with dosage. This may artificially inflate identifiability relative to a real-world scenario where exposure must be estimated independently per model-item pair.\n\n4. **SFT injection is a weak proxy for real-world contamination.** The paper acknowledges this (Limitation 3), but the severity is understated. Real contamination occurs through pre-training data curation, not LoRA fine-tuning. LoRA SFT at rank 16 with all 7 projection modules produces a very different memorization profile than pre-training exposure -- it catastrophically destroys 3B GSM8K CoT reasoning (60.5% to 12.0%, D018), introduces position bias at d005 (D019), and creates format-dependent evaluation artifacts (D017). These are not minor implementation issues; they fundamentally question whether the contamination effect measured by gamma generalizes to natural contamination. The paper should more prominently discuss what the Qwen2.5-Instruct observational results (Tables 15-16) actually show about ecological validity.\n\n5. **MMLU required subsampling to 1000 items due to NUTS infeasibility.** Decision D023 documents that the 4PL+gamma model on full MMLU (14,042 items, ~56K parameters) produced zero samples after 6 hours of NUTS warmup. The adopted strategy subsamples 1000 items and uses fitted gamma to infer over the full set, citing TinyBenchmarks (Polo et al. 2024) as precedent. However, TinyBenchmarks addresses a fundamentally different problem (efficient evaluation, not contamination calibration), and the validity of extrapolating item parameters from a subsample to out-of-sample items is not established. This limitation should be prominently disclosed, and the MMLU results (MAE 1.1pp) should be interpreted with this caveat.\n\n6. **Single model family with limited cross-family validation.** The controlled experiments use only Qwen2.5, with Llama-3.2-1B as the sole cross-family check. The Llama validation (Table 13) shows gamma Pearson r=0.91 with Qwen estimates and MAE of 2.1pp, but this is a single model at a single scale. The high gamma correlation could reflect shared training data or similar architectures rather than method generalizability. The paper needs multi-family validation at multiple scales to support the generalization claim, or should scope claims accordingly.\n\n### Questions for Authors\n\n1. **Gamma bias characterization**: Your internal diagnostics show +23-58% positive gamma bias at N=30-50 models. The paper reports 1.8pp MAE -- can you decompose this into the contributions from gamma estimation bias vs. theta estimation error? Specifically, if you run the calibration with true gamma (from ground truth dosage labels) instead of estimated gamma, how much does MAE improve? This would isolate whether the calibration accuracy comes from accurate gamma estimation or from compensating errors in the joint posterior.\n\n2. **Exposure matrix rank structure**: The current `build_exposure_matrix` creates a rank-1 matrix (outer product of dosage and item RRF scores). Have you experimented with using per-model-item RRF scores directly as the exposure matrix? If so, how does gamma recovery change? The rank-1 structure means gamma is essentially being estimated from the dosage-accuracy regression with item weights -- it may be identifiable only because dosage is known by design.\n\n3. **Coverage calibration at different dosage levels**: The 94% aggregate coverage is impressive, but is it uniform across dosage levels? Specifically, what is the coverage for d100 models (where Table 8 shows a 4.2pp residual at 3B GSM8K) vs. d000 models (where contamination effect should be zero)? Overdispersed credible intervals at low dosage could mask undercoverage at high dosage.\n\n4. **Local independence residual correlations**: You report mean r=0.032, max r=0.085 for item residual correlations. How were these computed -- from the posterior predictive distribution or from raw residuals? For N=45 models, even under true local independence, the sampling distribution of pairwise item correlations has non-trivial variance. Have you compared your observed residual correlation distribution against a null distribution from simulation under true local independence?\n\n5. **Closed-weight degradation**: Table 14 shows the 2-signal variant (S1+S3 only) achieves 2.1-2.8pp MAE at 1.5B+, \"matching DCR.\" But DCR does not require access to model internals at all. Under what practical scenario would a user have access to embedding similarity (S1, which requires a training data index) but not model logits (for S2 Min-K++)? The closed-weight framing seems to conflate \"no logit access\" with \"no training data access.\"\n\n### Minor Issues\n\n1. The RRF k=60 is taken directly from Cormack et al. (2009) without justification for this specific application. A sensitivity analysis on k (e.g., k in {10, 30, 60, 120}) would strengthen the aggregation design choice.\n\n2. The paper uses \"calibration\" in the contamination-correction sense throughout, but should more clearly disambiguate from probability calibration (Guo et al. 2017) in the introduction, as NLP reviewers will likely conflate the two.\n\n3. Table 4 shows S1-only achieves AUC 0.979/0.972/0.964 across benchmarks at 0.5B, *outperforming* 4-signal RRF (0.962/0.924/0.943) due to noise dilution. This undermines the RRF contribution claim -- at the only scale where controlled experiments are complete, a single signal beats fusion.\n\n4. The Fisher Information derivation for I(gamma_k) is described as \"derived\" but the paper framing document (D008) flags this as a \"critical gap\" across all three dream review tiers, suggesting it may be stated without proof in the main text. The derivation should appear at minimum in the appendix with explicit assumptions.\n\n5. The 3B GSM8K exclusion (D018) due to LoRA-induced CoT catastrophic forgetting is mentioned as a limitation, but the d000 SFT baseline dropping from 60.5% to 12.0% raises broader concerns about whether the LoRA fine-tuning setup is appropriate for *any* generative benchmark at this scale.\n\n6. The bootstrap CIs on MAE ([1.2, 2.4]) are computed by resampling model-benchmark pairs. With only 45 instances per benchmark (and some shared across dosage levels), the effective sample size for the bootstrap may be too small for reliable CI estimation. Cluster bootstrap by model identity would be more appropriate.\n\n### Preliminary Score: 4/6\n### Confidence: 4/5\n\nThe paper addresses a genuinely important problem with a well-grounded methodological approach. The per-benchmark gamma_k finding and signal failure characterization are valuable contributions. However, the identified gamma bias at realistic sample sizes, the discrepancy between claimed and actual measurement error model usage, the rank-1 exposure matrix construction, and the reliance on a single model family for controlled experiments collectively limit confidence in the reported results. With revisions addressing the identifiability bias disclosure, exposure matrix construction, and clearer ME model presentation, this could become a solid contribution. In its current form, it is above average but falls short of the standard for acceptance at a top venue."}],"totalDurationMs":163835,"totalTokens":90322,"totalToolUseCount":33,"usage":{"input_tokens":3,"cache_creation_input_tokens":697,"cache_read_input_tokens":86410,"output_tokens":3212,"server_tool_use":{"web_search_requests":0,"web_fetch_requests":0},"service_tier":"standard","cache_creation":{"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":697},"inference_geo":"","iterations":[{"input_tokens":3,"output_tokens":3212,"cache_read_input_tokens":86410,"cache_creation_input_tokens":697,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":697,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0},"type":"message"}],"speed":"standard"},"toolStats":{"readCount":12,"searchCount":9,"bashCount":0,"editFileCount":0,"linesAdded":0,"linesRemoved":0,"otherToolCount":12}}}
