{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01WjGgFnKYdC1t8wueh5cHM2","type":"tool_result","content":[{"type":"text","text":"I now have a comprehensive understanding of the paper, its experimental status, prior dream reviews, and all supporting materials. Let me compose my review.\n\n## R2 Review: Novelty & Related Work\n\n### Summary\nContamCal proposes a two-stage framework for calibrating benchmark scores under training data contamination: Stage 1 fuses four detection signals via RRF into per-item exposure scores; Stage 2 embeds these as covariates in a contamination-augmented 4PL IRT model with per-benchmark susceptibility parameters and a measurement error model, producing calibrated scores with Bayesian credible intervals. The paper reframes contamination as a confounder to statistically adjust for rather than eliminate, drawing on item compromise IRT from educational measurement. The signal effectiveness characterization across scales is an empirically useful secondary contribution.\n\n### Strengths\n\n1. **The reframing is the right intellectual move.** The shift from contamination elimination to statistical adjustment is well-motivated by Xu et al. 2024's finding that all 20 mitigation strategies fail. Positioning ContamCal as a \"statistical safety net\" rather than a solution is intellectually honest and fills a genuine gap: the detection-to-calibration bridge is real and unoccupied. This is the kind of conceptual contribution that can redirect a subfield's energy.\n\n2. **Grounding in item compromise IRT is well-chosen and differentiated.** The connection to Eckerly 2017 and Sinharay 2017 is not superficial -- the problem structure (leaked test items, inflated scores, need to recover true ability) genuinely parallels LLM benchmark contamination. The three structural adaptations (independent examinees to single model, binary to continuous exposure, stable to regime-conditional ability) are well-articulated and demonstrate genuine engagement with the psychometric literature, even if their individual novelty varies (see Weaknesses).\n\n3. **Per-benchmark gamma_k is the correct parameterization and yields an interpretable finding.** The 4x heterogeneity between GSM8K (gamma=0.85) and MMLU/ARC-C (gamma~0.2) is a clean, interpretable result. The cross-family consistency (Qwen-Llama Pearson r=0.91) suggesting contamination susceptibility is a benchmark property rather than a model property is a finding with implications beyond this paper. This transforms gamma_k from a nuisance parameter into a benchmark characterization tool.\n\n4. **The Fisher Information derivation is rigorous and well-structured.** The appendix provides complete proofs via both score variance and Bernoulli information formula, with the AUC-Information relationship (Corollary 1) and measurement error attenuation (Corollary 2) adding genuine theoretical depth. The distinction between structural identifiability (AUC >= 0.55) and practical identifiability (AUC >= 0.70) is principled. This is the strongest theoretical contribution.\n\n5. **Meta-circularity argument for RRF is sound and unusually honest.** Most papers would claim a learned aggregator as a contribution. Correctly identifying that a learned aggregator's training data may itself be contaminated, and therefore choosing training-free RRF as a principled design decision rather than positioning it as a contribution, demonstrates good scientific judgment. The empirical result that calibrated-weighted RRF only marginally improves over standard RRF (0.931 vs 0.924 at 1.5B) validates this choice.\n\n6. **Min-K++ directional reversal is a cautionary finding with independent value.** The discovery that Min-K++ actively misleads at high contamination dosage (Cohen's d = -1.22 to -2.67) is important for the broader detection community. This goes beyond \"signal fails\" to \"signal anti-correlates,\" which has direct practical implications for any pipeline using Min-K++ scores uncritically.\n\n### Weaknesses\n\n1. **The core methodological novelty is limited -- this is a competent synthesis, not a new technique.** Equation 3 (the 4PL+gamma model) adds a single linear contamination covariate to standard 4PL IRT. This is a direct, almost textbook application of explanatory IRT (De Boeck & Wilson 2004) and item compromise IRT (Eckerly 2017). RRF is off-the-shelf (Cormack et al. 2009). The measurement error model is classical errors-in-variables. Bayesian inference via NUTS is routine. For a top venue at 25% acceptance, synthesis papers must demonstrate that the combination produces emergent value beyond what a competent practitioner would obtain by assembling the same components. The headline MAE of 1.8pp is impressive, but how much of this is attributable to the IRT modeling versus simply having good detection signals? The 2PL+gamma baseline (MAE 2.9pp) already captures most of the calibration benefit, suggesting that the 4PL machinery and measurement error model provide only incremental improvement. The paper would be strengthened by a clearer analysis of where the IRT modeling adds value over simpler approaches (e.g., a well-calibrated linear model with the same exposure scores).\n\n2. **The causal inference analogy (Rosenbaum & Rubin 1983) is suggestive but never formalized.** The introduction draws an explicit parallel to observational causal inference, but the paper provides no DAG, no formal identification argument for the counterfactual quantity theta* (what ability would the model have absent contamination), and no sensitivity analysis for unmeasured confounding. What about contamination channels undetected by all four signals? Pre-training contamination may not produce detectable signals via any of the four methods, yet systematically inflate scores. The propensity score analogy breaks down because there is no equivalent of the strong ignorability assumption -- we cannot argue that conditional on the four signals, remaining contamination is independent of item responses. The paper should either formalize the causal argument or downgrade it from the introduction's framing to a looser motivational analogy in the related work.\n\n3. **Adaptation 2 (binary to continuous exposure) is standard in explanatory IRT and should not be claimed as a structural contribution.** De Boeck & Wilson (2004) explicitly cover continuous covariates in IRT. The measurement error model is the genuinely non-trivial part (propagating noisy signal estimates), but the continuous-vs-binary distinction per se is not novel. Similarly, Adaptation 3 (regime-conditional ability) reduces to using different measurement error variances for different training regimes -- a parametric choice, not a structural innovation. Of the three adaptations, only Adaptation 1 (single model responding to all items, altering local independence) represents a genuinely non-standard setting, and the paper acknowledges this is only mitigated, not solved.\n\n4. **The \"scale-dependent\" signal effectiveness claim rests on a confound between model capacity and LoRA adapter size.** The paper attributes S2/S4 failure at 0.5B to \"insufficient capacity\" for memorization detection. But LoRA adapter sizes grow proportionally with model size (34MB at 0.5B to 115MB at 3B), so the training intervention itself differs across scales. Does S2 fail because small models have insufficient capacity for detectable memorization, or because the LoRA adapter at 0.5B modifies a smaller fraction of the representation space? This confound is not discussed. A more controlled experiment would fix the adapter-to-parameter ratio across scales, which would disambiguate capacity from intervention effects. As stated, \"scale-dependent\" could also mean \"intervention-dependent.\"\n\n5. **SFT contamination injection is a weak proxy for real-world contamination, and the observational studies cannot compensate.** The controlled experiments use LoRA SFT on paraphrased benchmark items, but real contamination occurs during pre-training (bulk data mixing) or emerges through reinforcement learning on overlapping data. The SFT mechanism produces qualitatively different memorization patterns -- more surface-level, more detectable, potentially more amenable to calibration. The observational studies on Instruct and DeepSeek models (Section 5.6) show small adjustments (0.4-1.6pp) with wide CIs, but without ground truth, we cannot assess whether these adjustments are accurate or whether the method is simply underadjusting for RL-regime contamination. The paper honestly acknowledges this limitation, but reviewers should weigh it seriously: the headline results (1.8pp MAE) are obtained entirely in the SFT regime where signals are strongest and most detectable.\n\n6. **Important related work gap: no engagement with data attribution methods as exposure signals.** TracIn (Pruthi et al. 2020) and TRAK (Park et al. 2023) are cited only in the conclusion as future work. These methods provide principled, gradient-based influence estimates that could serve as a fifth (or replacement) signal with stronger theoretical grounding than some of the current four. More substantively, the paper does not discuss why existing data attribution methods were not included -- is it computational cost, or does the meta-circularity argument apply differently? For a paper claiming comprehensive multi-signal contamination detection, the omission of influence function methods deserves explicit justification rather than a one-sentence future work mention.\n\n### Questions for Authors\n\n1. **How sensitive is the 1.8pp MAE to the choice of reference model for Min-K++?** Decision D016 specifies using the 0%-dosage fine-tuned model rather than the base model. What happens if the base model is used instead? Given Min-K++'s directional reversal at high dosage, the reference model choice could substantially affect Stage 1 exposure estimates and propagate through to Stage 2 calibration.\n\n2. **Can you provide the ablation showing ContamCal's performance with only embedding similarity (S1) as the signal?** At 0.5B, RRF degrades to S1-dominant mode (89% weight share). If S1-only performance is comparable to full ContamCal at 0.5B, this would suggest the multi-signal architecture is providing value only at larger scales, which would clarify the method's scope of applicability.\n\n3. **The local independence assumption is violated by construction. How large would residual correlations need to be before the IRT model breaks down?** The paper reports mean r=0.032 and max r=0.085, but provides no theoretical or empirical threshold for when these correlations would invalidate the credible intervals. At what correlation level would the 94% coverage degrade substantially?\n\n4. **The paper fits per-benchmark models. How would a hierarchical model with partial pooling of gamma_k across benchmarks perform?** This would allow borrowing strength across benchmarks while permitting heterogeneity, potentially improving estimation at small N. The choice of fully separate models versus partial pooling is not discussed.\n\n5. **What is the computational cost of the full pipeline?** The paper mentions 45 minutes per-benchmark MCMC, but the total pipeline includes signal computation (embedding indexing, Min-K++ forward passes, paraphrase generation, self-critique) for all model-item pairs. For a practitioner considering adoption, the total wall-clock time and GPU hours would be informative.\n\n### Minor Issues\n\n1. The \"dream\" paper (best-tier) reports specific numerical results (e.g., 1.8pp MAE, 94% coverage, gamma estimates) that appear in the abstract, but the actual paper draft has PLACEHOLDER markers throughout the experiments section. The review summary presented to me contains the dream numbers. If these are projected rather than empirical, the precise digits should be treated as aspirational.\n\n2. Table 1 in the positioning comparison includes a footnote dagger on ContamCal's RL-robust column (\"?†\"), stating \"Designed for RL robustness via uncertainty propagation; empirical validation pending (exp-008).\" This is appropriately honest, but the preceding related work text in Section 2.1 states that Self-Critique \"partially survives\" RL -- this claim comes from cited prior work (Wust et al. 2025), not from ContamCal's own experiments. The distinction should be clearer.\n\n3. The ConStat signal (S3) requires generating paraphrases, which is itself a potential source of contamination circularity -- if the paraphrase generator (Qwen2.5-7B-Instruct) has seen the benchmark items, the paraphrases may not be truly independent. This is acknowledged nowhere.\n\n4. The related work section is well-structured with six subsections, but the DIF paragraph could benefit from more explicit comparison: how exactly does gamma_k differ from a Mantel-Haenszel DIF parameter? The current text says \"continuous exposure scores rather than discrete demographic categories\" but does not discuss whether the statistical properties (power, Type I error control) carry over.\n\n5. Reference count is adequate (~28 in the main text, more in the dream version) but on the lower end for a paper spanning detection, IRT, Bayesian inference, and calibration literatures. The HELM benchmark suite (Liang et al. 2022) and Chatbot Arena (Zheng et al. 2023) -- both contamination-relevant evaluation paradigms -- are missing.\n\n### Preliminary Score: 4/6\n### Confidence: 4/5\n\n**Rationale**: The paper addresses an important and timely problem with a well-designed framework grounded in the correct psychometric tradition. The per-benchmark gamma_k finding and signal effectiveness characterization have independent value. However, the core methodological novelty is limited -- this is fundamentally a careful synthesis of existing techniques (explanatory IRT, RRF, errors-in-variables) applied to a new problem setting. For a top venue at 25% acceptance, I would need to see either (a) evidence that the combination produces emergent capabilities beyond what the components individually provide, or (b) a more complete set of empirical results demonstrating generalization beyond LoRA-SFT contamination on a single model family. The paper is currently between borderline reject and weak accept; the experimental results (if they match the projected quality) could push it toward acceptance, but the novelty ceiling remains a concern. The Fisher Information appendix and honest treatment of limitations are noteworthy positives that partially compensate for the synthesis nature of the contribution."},{"type":"text","text":"agentId: a15aded41d95e23d8 (use SendMessage with to: 'a15aded41d95e23d8' to continue this agent)\n<usage>total_tokens: 95449\ntool_uses: 20\nduration_ms: 148097</usage>"}]}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"49fcde5e-0610-4e7e-a894-56110a3c51ee","uuid":"c8222d13-6818-4788-b7a7-6d3e56b4896a","timestamp":"2026-05-14T18:26:58.942Z","tool_use_result":{"status":"completed","prompt":"You are Reviewer R2 for a top ML venue review (acceptance rate ~25%, 6-point scale: 1=Strong Reject, 6=Strong Accept). Your focus areas are **Novelty/Originality** and **Related Work/Positioning**.\n\n## Paper Summary\n\n**Title**: ContamCal: Calibrating Benchmark Scores for Training Data Contamination via Contamination-Augmented Item Response Theory\n\n**Core idea**: A two-stage framework bridging contamination detection and score calibration. Stage 1 computes item-level contamination exposure via 4 detection signals (embedding similarity, Min-K++, ConStat, LLM self-critique) aggregated through RRF. Stage 2 fits a contamination-augmented 4PL IRT model with per-benchmark γ_k and a measurement error model. Key reframing: rather than trying to eliminate contamination, statistically control for it.\n\n**Key results**: 1.8pp MAE (bootstrap 95% CI [1.2, 2.4]) with 94% credible interval coverage across Qwen2.5 (0.5B-7B) and Llama-3.2-1B, 3 benchmarks, 5 dosages, 3 seeds.\n\n## Paper Structure (13 pages)\n\n### Abstract & Introduction\n- Frames contamination as a confounder to be statistically adjusted rather than eliminated\n- Draws analogy to causal inference (Rosenbaum & Rubin 1983)\n- Three contributions: (1) ContamCal framework, (2) systematic signal characterization, (3) dual-track validation\n\n### Related Work (Section 2, ~1 page)\nSix subsections:\n1. **Contamination Detection**: Min-K++ (Shi et al. 2024), ConStat (Dekoninck et al. 2024), KDS (Zhang et al. 2024), CDD (Li et al. 2024b), Yang et al. 2023 self-critique. \"These methods flag items but do not produce calibrated scores.\"\n2. **IRT for LLM Evaluation**: Lalor et al. 2024, Polo et al. 2024, White et al. 2024, Zhuang et al. 2024. \"None model contamination as a covariate.\" Builds on explanatory IRT (De Boeck & Wilson 2004, Adams et al. 1997) and item compromise (Eckerly 2017, Sinharay 2017).\n3. **Contamination Mitigation**: DCR (Dong et al. 2024), Xu et al. 2024 survey (20 strategies all fail), LiveBench (White et al. 2024). \"Our approach is complementary.\"\n4. **Calibration and Uncertainty**: Distinguished from probability calibration (Guo et al. 2017, Platt 1999). Conformal prediction (Angelopoulos & Bates 2023) discussed but IRT preferred for interpretability.\n5. **Differential Item Functioning**: γ_k conceptually related to DIF (Holland & Wainer 1993, Thissen et al. 1993, Zumbo 1999). Key distinction: DIF compares demographic subgroups; this compares contamination exposure levels of items within a single model.\n\n### Method (Section 3)\n- Stage 1: 4 signals + RRF aggregation (Cormack et al. 2009)\n- Stage 2: 4PL+γ IRT with measurement error model, per-benchmark γ_k, Fisher Information analysis for identifiability\n- Calibrated score = expected accuracy under zero exposure\n\n### Key Technical Innovations\n1. Contamination as IRT covariate (γ_k·τ_i term in difficulty)\n2. Per-benchmark γ_k (heterogeneous contamination susceptibility)\n3. Measurement error model propagating signal noise to IRT posterior\n4. Fisher Information identifiability analysis (AUC≥0.55 structural, ≥0.70 practical)\n5. Training-free RRF aggregation avoiding meta-circularity\n\n### Experiments\n- Controlled: Qwen2.5 (0.5B-3B) × 5 dosages × 3 seeds × 3 benchmarks + 7B + Llama-3.2-1B\n- 7 baselines: Raw, Remove-rescore, 2PL+γ (S1 only), Vanilla 4PL, Linear+boot, Emperor mitigation, DCR fuzzy\n- Observational: Qwen2.5-Instruct, DeepSeek-R1, Qwen2.5-72B\n- Signal analysis: AUC mapping, Min-K++ reversal, RRF ablation\n- Simulation: 120-condition identifiability sweep\n\n### References\n~28 references. Key prior work cited: Shi et al. 2024 (Min-K++), Dekoninck et al. 2024 (ConStat), Dong et al. 2024 (DCR), Lalor et al. 2024 & Polo et al. 2024 (IRT for LLMs), De Boeck & Wilson 2004 (explanatory IRT), Eckerly 2017 & Sinharay 2017 (item compromise), Holland & Wainer 1993 (DIF).\n\n### Limitations\nModel family breadth, unidimensional θ, SFT≠real contamination, local independence, closed-weight degradation, computational cost.\n\n## Your Review Task\n\nProvide a detailed review focusing on:\n\n1. **Novelty Assessment**: How novel is the core idea of using contamination as an IRT covariate? How novel are the individual components (signal fusion, measurement error model, Fisher analysis)? Is this a genuine conceptual advance or incremental combination?\n\n2. **Related Work Coverage**: Are key prior works cited? Are there important missing references? Is the positioning accurate and fair?\n\n3. **Framing and Claims**: Is the causal inference analogy appropriate? Are the three claims well-supported? Is the paper overselling or underselling?\n\n4. **Broader Impact and Significance**: Would this change how the community thinks about contamination? Is the practical impact significant?\n\n5. **Weaknesses in Positioning**: Where does the novelty argument break down? What are the strongest counterarguments?\n\n## Output Format\n\n```\n## R2 Review: Novelty & Related Work\n\n### Summary\n[2-3 sentences]\n\n### Strengths\n[Numbered list, 4-6 items]\n\n### Weaknesses\n[Numbered list, 4-6 items]\n\n### Questions for Authors\n[Numbered list, 3-5 items]\n\n### Minor Issues\n[Numbered list]\n\n### Preliminary Score: X/6\n### Confidence: X/5\n```\n","agentId":"a15aded41d95e23d8","agentType":"general-purpose","content":[{"type":"text","text":"I now have a comprehensive understanding of the paper, its experimental status, prior dream reviews, and all supporting materials. Let me compose my review.\n\n## R2 Review: Novelty & Related Work\n\n### Summary\nContamCal proposes a two-stage framework for calibrating benchmark scores under training data contamination: Stage 1 fuses four detection signals via RRF into per-item exposure scores; Stage 2 embeds these as covariates in a contamination-augmented 4PL IRT model with per-benchmark susceptibility parameters and a measurement error model, producing calibrated scores with Bayesian credible intervals. The paper reframes contamination as a confounder to statistically adjust for rather than eliminate, drawing on item compromise IRT from educational measurement. The signal effectiveness characterization across scales is an empirically useful secondary contribution.\n\n### Strengths\n\n1. **The reframing is the right intellectual move.** The shift from contamination elimination to statistical adjustment is well-motivated by Xu et al. 2024's finding that all 20 mitigation strategies fail. Positioning ContamCal as a \"statistical safety net\" rather than a solution is intellectually honest and fills a genuine gap: the detection-to-calibration bridge is real and unoccupied. This is the kind of conceptual contribution that can redirect a subfield's energy.\n\n2. **Grounding in item compromise IRT is well-chosen and differentiated.** The connection to Eckerly 2017 and Sinharay 2017 is not superficial -- the problem structure (leaked test items, inflated scores, need to recover true